---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/pages/PageHead.astro';
import PageFoot from '../components/pages/PageFoot.astro';
import BoxDiagram from '../components/pages/BoxDiagram.astro';
import Ledger from '../components/pages/Ledger.astro';

const docs = [
  { href: '/docs/remote/', label: 'Remote boxes' },
  { href: '/docs/guides/remote-setup/', label: 'Preparing a box' },
  { href: '/docs/guides/remote-command/', label: 'Boxes reached by a command' },
  { href: '/docs/guides/remote-multiple-boxes/', label: 'Several boxes with the same project' },
  { href: '/docs/security/', label: 'Security' }
];

const ledger = [
  {
    title: 'The usual way',
    items: [
      {
        code: 'http://10.0.3.14:20011',
        text: 'A private IP in the address bar. Cookies, CORS origins and OAuth callbacks were written for a hostname.'
      },
      {
        code: 'ssh -L 20011:localhost:20011 my-vps',
        text: 'One forward per port, typed again after every sleep. The database client needs its own.'
      },
      {
        code: '*.dev.example.com, a wildcard cert, a subdomain per branch',
        text: 'DNS, certbot and a proxy config to edit every time a worktree appears.'
      },
      {
        code: 'https://random-words.example.dev',
        text: 'A public URL for something that should never have left your machine.'
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    title: 'With devlanes',
    items: [
      {
        code: 'devlanes remote connect my-vps --background',
        text: 'One command. The connection belongs to your local daemon and stays until you disconnect.'
      },
      {
        code: 'https://myapp1.localhost',
        text: "The box's hostnames join your local proxy with your local certificate. Nothing is public."
      },
      {
        code: 'psql -h 127.0.0.1 -p 20013 myapp_lane1',
        text: "Raw TCP works unchanged: databases, debuggers, gRPC, websockets, under the box's own port number."
      },
      {
        code: 'devlanes daemon pair',
        text: 'A one-time code pairs your machine. The box keeps a hash of the token, never the token.'
      }
    ]
  }
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Remote boxes"
  description="Run the lanes on a VPS, a container or a codespace and open them from your laptop. devlanes mirrors the box's hostnames and TCP ports over one ssh session; the box opens no ports."
  path="/remote/"
  current="remote"
>
  <PageHead title="The lanes run on a box. Your laptop gets their hostnames and ports.">
    <p>
      A box is any machine that runs devlanes and that you can reach with <code>ssh</code>, or with
      a command such as <code>docker exec</code>. Your laptop's daemon reads the box's lanes over
      one ssh session and mirrors them, with the same names and the same port numbers. The
      connection never runs commands on the box and never carries your environment.
    </p>
  </PageHead>

  <Section id="pair" title="Pair once. The box opens no ports.">
    <p>
      On the box, <code>devlanes daemon pair</code> prints a code that works once and expires in 10
      minutes. Your laptop trades it for a token the box keeps only a hash of, and presents that
      token on every later connection.
    </p>
    <p>
      The connection belongs to your local daemon, not to the terminal. It reconnects after sleep or
      network loss and comes back when the daemon restarts, until you disconnect. Over the ssh
      session devlanes runs <code>devlanes tunnel --stdio</code> on the box, which needs only a
      session channel; <code>AllowTcpForwarding no</code> there is fine.
    </p>
    <div slot="visual" class="terminal-stack">
      <Terminal title="on my-vps">
        <pre class="terminal-content"><span class="command">$ devlanes daemon pair</span>
pairing code: <span class="url">K7QM-3XWD</span>
it works once and expires in 10 minutes
on your other machine, run:
  devlanes remote connect &lt;box&gt; --code K7QM-3XWD</pre>
      </Terminal>
      <Terminal title="on your laptop">
        <pre class="terminal-content terminal-scroll"><span class="command">$ devlanes remote connect my-vps --code K7QM-3XWD --background</span>
<span class="command">$ devlanes url ls</span>
HOST                      TARGET       PORT   STATE
api.myapp1.localhost  ->  myapp@1:api  20010  <span class="success">ready</span>
myapp1.localhost      ->  myapp@1:web  20011  <span class="success">ready</span></pre>
      </Terminal>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="mirror" title="Same port numbers, same hostnames." stacked>
    <p>
      Every service on the box listens on loopback, on a port devlanes allocated, under a hostname
      devlanes generated. Your laptop's daemon binds those port numbers on its own 127.0.0.1 and
      adds the hostnames to its own proxy, signed by the CA you already trust. Raw TCP works
      unchanged: <code>psql</code> against the box's Postgres, a debugger, gRPC, websockets. Only
      TCP; UDP ports are not mirrored.
    </p>
    <BoxDiagram slot="visual" />
  </Section>

  <Section id="tui" title="The box's lane shows up in your TUI, read-only.">
    <p>
      A connected box's lanes join the tab of the project with the same name, after your own. The
      pane for a box lane lists its domain, each port and whether it is mirrored here, and each
      hostname with the URL that opens it on this machine.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keys that would start, stop or restart do nothing for a box lane. The pane says what to run on
      the box instead, and the monitor shows only this machine's units.
    </p>
    <Terminal slot="visual" title="devlanes, lane @1 on my-vps">
      <pre class="terminal-content terminal-scroll tui-text"><span class="url">devlanes</span>  <span class="dim">1 myapp</span>               <span class="dim">Monitor  Daemon  Boxes</span>
<span class="dim">lanes</span>  <span class="dim">1 @0 main</span>  <span class="tui-selected"> 2 @1 my-vps </span>     <span class="dim">[ ] switch lanes</span>
<span class="dim">┌</span> myapp@1  running · 3 up <span class="dim">────────────────</span> <span class="url">on my-vps</span> <span class="dim">┐</span>
<span class="dim">│</span> <span class="dim">box</span>       my-vps · ssh · devlanes 0.9.2            <span class="dim">│</span>
<span class="dim">│</span> <span class="dim">domain</span>    myapp1.localhost                         <span class="dim">│</span>
<span class="dim">│</span> <span class="dim">services</span>  3 of 3 up                                <span class="dim">│</span>
<span class="dim">│</span> <span class="dim">infra</span>     postgres :20013                          <span class="dim">│</span>
<span class="dim">│</span> <span class="dim">ports</span>     4 of 4 mirrored here                     <span class="dim">│</span>
<span class="dim">│</span> <span class="dim">urls</span>      <span class="url">https://api.myapp1.localhost</span>             <span class="dim">│</span>
<span class="dim">│</span>           <span class="url">https://myapp1.localhost</span>                 <span class="dim">│</span>
<span class="dim">│</span>                                                    <span class="dim">│</span>
<span class="dim">│</span> <span class="dim">Read-only here. On my-vps: ssh my-vps, then</span>        <span class="dim">│</span>
<span class="dim">│</span> <span class="dim">devlanes status myapp@1</span>                            <span class="dim">│</span>
<span class="dim">└────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</span>
 o <span class="dim">open in browser</span>   [ ] <span class="dim">switch lane</span>   ? <span class="dim">all keys</span></pre>
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <Section id="command" title="A box without ssh is reached by a command." reverse>
    <p>
      A container, a pod, a WSL distribution or a codespace has no sshd, but each has a command that
      runs a program inside and passes stdin and stdout through: <code>docker exec</code>,{' '}
      <code>kubectl exec</code>, <code>wsl.exe</code>, <code>gh codespace ssh</code>. Name the box
      in your user config with that command and it connects like any ssh host. Pairing works the
      same way.
    </p>
    <p>
      The command must end in <code>devlanes tunnel --stdio</code> inside the box, and devlanes has
      to be installed there. There is no ControlMaster: every reconnect runs the command again.
    </p>
    <Terminal slot="visual" title="~/.devlanes/config.yaml">
      <pre class="terminal-content terminal-scroll"><span class="key">remote</span>:
  <span class="key">boxes</span>:
    <span class="key">myapp</span>:
      <span class="key">command</span>: [docker, exec, -i, myapp, devlanes, tunnel, --stdio]</pre>
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <Section id="ledger" title="The usual way, and with devlanes." stacked>
    <p>
      A dev server on a box was reachable before devlanes. Each way of getting to it either lost the
      hostname, had to be redone after every sleep, or made public a machine that should not be.
    </p>
    <Ledger slot="visual" columns={ledger} />
  </Section>

  <PageFoot links={docs} />
</SiteLayout>
